          %title Universal constructions
          %h2#UniversalConstructions Universal constructions
          %p
            What makes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            be
            = succeed "," do
              %em theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            with a prescribed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            which are
            %em universal
            with this property, in that they “know about” or “subsume” every other object with that same kind of property. Category theory allows to make precise what this means, and then to discover and prove theorems about it.
          %p
            Universal constructions are all over the place in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
          %p
            There are several different formulations of the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#LimitsAndColimits"} Limits and colimits
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#TopologicalEndsAndCoends"} Ends and coends
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#KanExtension"} Left and right Kan extensions
          %p
            But these three kinds of constructions all turn out to be special cases of each other, hence they really reflect different perspectives on a single topic of universal constructions. In fact, all three are also special cases of the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            (Def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointFunctorsInTermsOfNaturalBijectionOfHomSets"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            theory is really the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            and hence, if we follow (
            %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#Lambek82"}> Lambek 82
            ), of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#LimitsAndColimits Limits and colimits
          %p
            Maybe the most hands-on version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Limits"}
            below), which is short for
            %em
              limiting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cones"} cones
            (Remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LimitingCones"}
            below). The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} formally dual
            concept (Example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OppositeCategory"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            (which are hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            in an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
          %table{:style => "margin:auto"}
