                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{A}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+limit"} direct limit
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{A}
          %p
            There is a variety of different kinds of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            shape that they are limiting (co-)cones over. This includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+products"}> fiber products
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+limits"} filtered limits
            and various others, all of which are basic tools frequently used whenever
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            applies.
          %p
            A key fact of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoints+preserve+limits"} right adjoints preserve limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoints+preserve+colimits"} left adjoints preserve colimits
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointsPreserveCoLimits"}
            below). This will be used all the time. A partial converse to this statement is that if a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            preserves
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            is, if it exists, objectwise given by a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            under/over the given functor (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PointwiseExpressionOfLeftAdjoints"}
            below). Since these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+categories"} comma categories
            are in general not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small
            %em
